{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <cctype>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string input;\n    getline(cin, input);\n    \n    string result;\n    result.reserve(input.length());\n    \n    if (!input.empty()) {\n        result += input[0];\n    }\n    \n    bool at_replaced = false;\n    size_t i = 1;\n    \n    while (i < input.length()) {\n        if (i + 2 < input.length() && \n            input[i] == 'd' && input[i+1] == 'o' && input[i+2] == 't') {\n            result += '.';\n            i += 3;\n        }\n        else if (i + 1 < input.length() && \n                 !at_replaced && \n                 input[i] == 'a' && input[i+1] == 't') {\n            result += '@';\n            at_replaced = true;\n            i += 2;\n        }\n        else {\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    cout << result << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <cctype>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string input;\n    getline(cin, input);\n    \n    // We need to find all possible positions of 'dot' and 'at' substrings\n    // and choose replacements that minimize length while forming a valid email\n    \n    // First, let's collect all positions where 'dot' and 'at' occur\n    vector<int> dot_positions;\n    vector<int> at_positions;\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)input.length(); i++) {\n        if (i + 2 < input.length() && input[i] == 'd' && input[i+1] == 'o' && input[i+2] == 't') {\n            dot_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n        if (i + 1 < input.length() && input[i] == 'a' && input[i+1] == 't') {\n            at_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Try all possible combinations of replacements\n    // Since we want minimal length and lexicographically smallest, we need to be careful\n    \n    // The key insight: we need exactly one '@', and dots can be any number\n    // We should replace 'at' with '@' only once, and 'dot' with '.' as needed\n    \n    // Let's try to find the optimal replacement by considering all possible 'at' positions\n    string best_result = \"\";\n    int min_length = INT_MAX;\n    \n    // Try each 'at' position as the one to replace with '@'\n    for (int at_idx : at_positions) {\n        string candidate;\n        int pos = 0;\n        bool at_replaced = false;\n        \n        while (pos < input.length()) {\n            // Check if we're at a 'dot' position\n            bool is_dot = (pos + 2 < input.length() && \n                          input[pos] == 'd' && input[pos+1] == 'o' && input[pos+2] == 't');\n            \n            // Check if we're at the chosen 'at' position\n            bool is_at = (!at_replaced && pos + 1 < input.length() &&\n                         input[pos] == 'a' && input[pos+1] == 't' && pos == at_idx);\n            \n            if (is_dot) {\n                candidate += '.';\n                pos += 3;\n            } else if (is_at) {\n                candidate += '@';\n                at_replaced = true;\n                pos += 2;\n            } else {\n                candidate += input[pos];\n                pos++;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Check if candidate is a valid email\n        if (candidate.length() == 0) continue;\n        if (candidate[0] == '.' || candidate[0] == '@') continue;\n        if (candidate[candidate.length()-1] == '.' || candidate[candidate.length()-1] == '@') continue;\n        \n        // Count '@' symbols - must be exactly 1\n        int at_count = 0;\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (c == '@') at_count++;\n        }\n        if (at_count != 1) continue;\n        \n        // Check all characters are valid (lowercase letters, '.', '@')\n        bool valid = true;\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (!(c == '.' || c == '@' || (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z'))) {\n                valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!valid) continue;\n        \n        // Update best result\n        if (candidate.length() < min_length || \n            (candidate.length() == min_length && candidate < best_result)) {\n            min_length = candidate.length();\n            best_result = candidate;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Also consider case where no 'at' exists - but problem states we have to transform,\n    // and input should contain 'at' somewhere based on problem context\n    \n    // If no valid replacement found, try to handle edge cases\n    if (best_result.empty()) {\n        // Fallback: try to replace all 'dot' with '.' and see if we can make it valid\n        string candidate;\n        int pos = 0;\n        while (pos < input.length()) {\n            if (pos + 2 < input.length() && \n                input[pos] == 'd' && input[pos+1] == 'o' && input[pos+2] == 't') {\n                candidate += '.';\n                pos += 3;\n            } else {\n                candidate += input[pos];\n                pos++;\n            }\n        }\n        // This fallback might not work for all cases, but problem context suggests valid input\n        best_result = candidate;\n    }\n    \n    cout << best_result << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-9.536738616588991e-07, -8.844937838148326e-05, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -4.410734163684538e-06, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, -0.00010251473577227443, 0.0, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.012512397021055222, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.12156524509191513, -3.099436753473128e-06, -1.6689286894688848e-06, -0.6931861639022827, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -2.145764938177308e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.008705285377800465, -4.0531076592742465e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.00013076403411105275, -4.0531076592742465e-